## Proof

Let $N = n_1 + n_2 + \cdots + n_s$, and consider the monic polynomial
$$P(x; r_1,\dots,r_s) = \prod_{i=1}^{s}(x - r_i)^{n_i}.$$
Writing $P(x) = x^N + a_{N-1}x^{N-1} + \cdots + a_0$, the coefficient map is
$$F: (r_1,\dots,r_s) \longmapsto (a_0, a_1, \dots, a_{N-1}) \in \mathbb{R}^N \text{ (or } \mathbb{C}^N\text{)}.$$
The Jacobian $dF$ is an $N \times s$ matrix. Its $i$-th column is the vector of coefficients of the polynomial $\dfrac{\partial P}{\partial r_i}$ (viewed as a polynomial of degree $\le N-1$, since the leading coefficient $1$ is fixed). Hence
$$\operatorname{rank}(dF) = \dim \operatorname{span}\!\left\{\frac{\partial P}{\partial r_i} : 1 \le i \le s\right\}.$$

**Computing the partial derivatives.** Since only the factor $(x-r_i)^{n_i}$ depends on $r_i$,
$$\frac{\partial P}{\partial r_i} = -n_i\,(x-r_i)^{n_i-1}\prod_{j \ne i}(x-r_j)^{n_j} = -n_i\,\frac{P(x)}{x - r_i}.$$
Set $Q_i(x) := \dfrac{P(x)}{x - r_i} = (x-r_i)^{n_i-1}\displaystyle\prod_{j\ne i}(x-r_j)^{n_j}$, a polynomial of degree $N-1$. The $Q_i$ differ from the $\partial P/\partial r_i$ only by the nonzero scalar $-n_i$, so it suffices to prove:

> **Claim.** The polynomials $Q_1, \dots, Q_s$ are linearly independent whenever $r_1, \dots, r_s$ are pairwise distinct.

**Proof of Claim.** Suppose $\sum_{i=1}^{s} c_i\, Q_i(x) = 0$ identically. Fix any index $k \in \{1,\dots,s\}$; we show $c_k = 0$.

For $i \ne k$, the factor $\prod_{j \ne i}(x - r_j)^{n_j}$ contains $(x - r_k)^{n_k}$, so $Q_i(x) = (x - r_k)^{n_k}\, R_i(x)$ for some polynomial $R_i$. Taking the $(n_k - 1)$-st derivative and evaluating at $x = r_k$: by the Leibniz rule,
$$D^{n_k-1}\bigl[(x-r_k)^{n_k} R_i(x)\bigr]\Big|_{x=r_k} = \sum_{m=0}^{n_k-1}\binom{n_k-1}{m}\frac{n_k!}{(n_k-m)!}(x-r_k)^{n_k-m}\,D^{n_k-1-m}R_i(x)\Big|_{x=r_k} = 0,$$
since every summand retains a factor $(x - r_k)$ (as $m \le n_k - 1$ gives $n_k - m \ge 1$).

For $i = k$, we have $Q_k(x) = (x - r_k)^{n_k - 1}\prod_{j \ne k}(x - r_j)^{n_j}$. Taking the $(n_k-1)$-st derivative and evaluating at $r_k$: the factor $(x-r_k)^{n_k-1}$ contributes exactly $(n_k-1)!$, and the remaining factor $\prod_{j\ne k}(x-r_j)^{n_j}$ is untouched (its derivatives of order $\le n_k - 1$ evaluated at $r_k$ only need the zeroth-order term, since all higher-order Leibniz terms still carry a surviving $(x-r_k)^{n_k-1-m}$ with $m\ge 1$... more directly: the only Leibniz term that survives at $x = r_k$ is the one where all $n_k-1$ derivatives hit $(x-r_k)^{n_k-1}$). Concretely,
$$D^{n_k-1}Q_k(x)\Big|_{x=r_k} = (n_k-1)!\prod_{j\ne k}(r_k - r_j)^{n_j} \ne 0,$$
because the $r_j$ are pairwise distinct.

Applying $D^{n_k-1}$ to the identity $\sum_i c_i Q_i = 0$ and evaluating at $x = r_k$, all $i \ne k$ terms vanish by the computation above, leaving
$$c_k\,(n_k-1)!\prod_{j\ne k}(r_k - r_j)^{n_j} = 0.$$
The product is nonzero (distinct roots), so $c_k = 0$.

Since $k$ was arbitrary, $c_1 = \cdots = c_s = 0$. ∎

**Conclusion.** The $s$ polynomials $\partial P/\partial r_i$ are linearly independent, so
$$\operatorname{rank}(dF) = s.$$

This is consistent with the dimension count: the domain has dimension $s$ and $s \le N = \sum n_i$, so the maximal possible rank $s$ is attained.

